  "page1": {
    "title": "The Sign That Counts the Roots",
    "subtitle": "One small number already knows how a quadratic equation will end.",
    "body": "Kick a ball into the air, and its height follows a smooth curve. Mathematicians draw curves like this with a <u>quadratic equation</u>, one in which the highest power of the unknown is the square. To solve such an equation, you find its roots, the values of x that make both sides equal. Some quadratic equations have two roots, some have only one, and some have no real root at all. How can you tell which case you face?\n\nLuckily, you do not have to solve the equation to find out. A small expression called the <u>discriminant</u> hides inside the quadratic formula. You compute it from the three coefficients of the equation: square the middle one, then subtract four times the product of the other two. The result is a single number, and its sign tells the whole story. The discriminant sits under a square root sign, and that spot gives it all its power.\n\nThink about the three cases. When the discriminant is positive, the square root is a real number, so the formula produces two different real roots. On a graph, the parabola crosses the x-axis at two separate points. When the discriminant is exactly zero, the square root vanishes, and the two roots collapse into one repeated root. Now the parabola touches the axis at only a single point. When the discriminant is negative, no real number works as its square root, so the equation has no real roots, and the curve never meets the axis.\n\nThis is why the discriminant matters so much. It works like a quick test that sorts every equation into one of three groups. Engineers use it to check whether a design equation has a real solution before they build anything. In each case, the sign of one small number reveals <blank>, long before anyone finishes the full calculation.",
    "illustration": "../../assets/illustrations/2026-08/02.jpg",
    "illustration_caption": "판별식의 부호에 따라 포물선은 x축과 두 점에서 만나거나, 한 점에서 접하거나, 아예 만나지 않는다.",
    "gloss_extra": [
      {
        "term": "coefficient",
        "ko": "계수 (문자 앞에 곱해진 수)"
      },
      {
        "term": "parabola",
        "ko": "포물선"
      },
      {
        "term": "vanish",
        "ko": "사라지다, 0이 되다"
      }
    ]
  },
